  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 423 AM CDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nThursday and Friday, guidance is indicating rounds of showers \nand thunderstorms will impact IA and northern IL, which will \nsend convection into northeastern MO and west central MO. There \nis support for development throughout the depth of the \natmosphere, with upper level divergence to the north of our \nregion and low-level warm air advection providing lift. NBM \nguidance gives a 60-80% chance of southeastern IA and extreme \nnortheastern MO of receiving at least 1 inch of rain in this \nsystem. The gradient associated with the possible rainfall \ntotals is very tight across the region. For example, just to the\nsouth of the max swath, NBM guidance indicates that there is a \n50-80% of northeastern MO and west central IL receiving at least\n0.25 inches of rain. To further illustrate this, the St. Louis \nmetropolitan area only has around a 30% chance of receiving 0.1 \nduring the same time. With the convective nature of this event \nand the coarse resolution of the model data, we can\u2019t rule out \nthe possibilities of locally higher totals depending on where \nthe swath of rain ends up.\n\nBetween the cold front and the ongoing rain/clouds by Thursday,\nnortheastern MO and west central IL have a higher potential of\nseeing slight relief from the heat by a few degrees. Although,\nit is still uncertain with maximum temperature IQRs of around 10\ndegrees. This wide range in values is the result of the\nuncertainty surrounding how far south the front will progress\nalong with the exact amount of rain and cloud cover. Regardless\nif such relief does occur, there is high confidence (80-90%)\nthat temperatures will rebound to the levels that they have been\nthe last couple days. This is a result of model guidance\nconsensus that the ridge will build back north, bringing\ncontinued heat for the entire region through Sunday. There are\nsigns of another cooldown in the guidance starting Monday. While\nexact values are not certain, the entire range of the IQRs for\nMonday\u2019s high temperatures is lower than the low end of the IQRs\nwe currently see for temperatures in the short term.",
